# Server and client compatibility

Full text and reasoning: `ПРАВИЛА-РАБОТЫ.md` §2–§4. Here is what must hold while
this code is being edited.

> **The server must keep working with an old client.**

The asymmetry is not a preference. There is one server and you control it. There
are many clients, on other people's phones, updating whenever they feel like it
or never. A broken old client cannot be reached, let alone fixed.

## Migrations are additive

- A new column carries `DEFAULT` or is `NULL`-able.
- Do not rename, drop, or narrow a `CHECK` in the same release as the code that
  depends on it. Removal goes **one release later**.
- Precedent in this repository: `0017` added encrypted columns beside the plain
  ones, `0018` dropped the plain ones a release later.

An empty-string default is often better than `NULL`: `devices.platform` and
`devices.name` use it so that rows created before the migration keep working
instead of forcing a branch in every consumer.

## The API only widens

Allowed: a new endpoint; a new **optional** request field; a new response field.

Not allowed: making an existing field required; changing what an existing field
means; removing a field from a response.

Precedent: `platform` arrived as an optional registration field plus
`PUT /devices/me/platform`, which the client calls at startup. Installations made
before the field keep working and **heal themselves** rather than being cut off.
Prefer that shape.

## Deployment order

**Server before client. Always, no exceptions decided locally.**

A server that understands both old and new is safe at any moment. A client that
requires an endpoint the server does not have yet is broken from the first
second.

## Irreversible changes ship alone

A migration that erases data does not travel with a feature. The feature ships
and proves itself first; erasure is a separate, deliberate deployment.

Every deployment needs an answer to "how do we roll this back". An additive one
has it: the new code simply goes unused. A destructive one does not — which is
exactly why it is separated.

## Two limits worth remembering here

**`devices.platform` is a policy guardrail, not a security boundary.** The device
reports its own platform, and until hardware attestation exists the server cannot
verify it. A modified client will call itself a phone. Do not write code that
relies on it as if it were proof.

**The server cannot rotate a group key.** `GK` is generated by the client;
the server only publishes `group.rotation_needed`. A revoked device keeps access
to the GK versions whose wrappers it already holds until an owner or admin
actually rotates. Any design that assumes server-side rotation is wrong.

## Skipped tests are not passing tests

Every test in `server/internal/api` goes through `setup(t)`, which calls
`t.Skipf` when the database is unreachable — and the package still prints `ok`
and exits zero. When changing anything here, confirm the tests actually ran:
count `SKIP` lines explicitly instead of trusting the package result.
